1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,527
Files are what a server is made of.

2
00:00:03,077 --> 00:00:03,657
Configs,

3
00:00:03,657 --> 00:00:04,020
logs,

4
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,254
scripts and data.

5
00:00:05,254 --> 00:00:07,286
Every task starts by making,

6
00:00:07,286 --> 00:00:07,867
reading,

7
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:09,391
moving or fixing one.

8
00:00:09,941 --> 00:00:12,821
Today you handle them safely, and you edit with vim.

9
00:00:14,871 --> 00:00:17,114
Build a practice area.

10
00:00:17,114 --> 00:00:20,172
Type: mkdir -p lab27/docs/old.

11
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:25,474
The dash p flag makes every parent folder in one go.

12
00:00:26,024 --> 00:00:29,458
Step inside. Type: cd lab27.

13
00:00:30,008 --> 00:00:31,277
Now empty files.

14
00:00:31,277 --> 00:00:33,815
Type: touch hello.txt notes.txt.

15
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:37,304
Touch creates a file when it does not exist.

16
00:00:37,854 --> 00:00:40,841
Check with: ls -l. Look at the size column.

17
00:00:41,391 --> 00:00:46,938
Zero bytes. Both files exist, and both are empty. A folder starts with the letter d.

18
00:00:48,988 --> 00:00:51,633
Bring in a file with real content.

19
00:00:51,633 --> 00:00:53,578
Type: cp ~/shopping.txt .

20
00:00:53,578 --> 00:00:54,590
with a space,

21
00:00:54,590 --> 00:00:55,446
then a dot.

22
00:00:55,446 --> 00:00:56,924
The dot means here.

23
00:00:57,474 --> 00:01:01,165
Read it all with: cat shopping.txt. Cat prints a file.

24
00:01:01,715 --> 00:01:07,347
For a quick peek, type: head -n 3 shopping.txt. Head shows the first lines.

25
00:01:07,897 --> 00:01:14,894
And the end of a file. Type: tail -n 2 shopping.txt. Tail is the one you will use on logs.

26
00:01:15,444 --> 00:01:22,633
How long is it? Type: wc -l shopping.txt. W C counts, and dash l counts lines.

27
00:01:23,183 --> 00:01:27,045
And what kind of file is it? Type: file shopping.txt.

28
00:01:27,595 --> 00:01:32,395
ASCII text. Linux does not trust the end of a name. It looks inside the file.

29
00:01:34,445 --> 00:01:40,098
Copy with: cp shopping.txt backup.txt. Source first, then destination.

30
00:01:40,648 --> 00:01:47,027
Move it into a folder. Type: mv backup.txt docs/. A move takes it out of here.

31
00:01:47,577 --> 00:01:54,553
The same command renames. Type: mv notes.txt ideas.txt. Same folder, new name.

32
00:01:55,103 --> 00:02:01,674
To copy a whole folder, add dash r, for recursive. Type: cp -r docs docs2.

33
00:02:02,224 --> 00:02:08,304
See everything. Type: ls -R. Capital R lists every folder underneath.

34
00:02:08,854 --> 00:02:12,694
Now delete, carefully. Type: rm hello.txt.

35
00:02:13,244 --> 00:02:14,278
No question,

36
00:02:14,278 --> 00:02:15,917
and no recycle bin.

37
00:02:15,917 --> 00:02:16,865
It is gone.

38
00:02:16,865 --> 00:02:17,297
Next,

39
00:02:17,297 --> 00:02:19,883
a folder and everything in it.

40
00:02:19,883 --> 00:02:21,436
Type: rm -r docs2.

41
00:02:21,986 --> 00:02:26,103
Check twice before you press Enter on that one. Now confirm with: ls.

42
00:02:28,153 --> 00:02:29,689
Four habits keep you safe.

43
00:02:30,239 --> 00:02:33,375
One. Run ls first, so you know what the name matches.

44
00:02:33,925 --> 00:02:38,448
Two. Never use a star with rm until you have run ls with the same star.

45
00:02:38,998 --> 00:02:43,520
Three. When unsure, use rm dash i. It asks before every delete.

46
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:46,865
Four. There is no undo. A backup is your undo.

47
00:02:47,415 --> 00:02:49,122
Look twice. Delete once.

48
00:02:51,172 --> 00:02:52,356
Lost a file?

49
00:02:52,356 --> 00:02:53,243
Use find.

50
00:02:53,243 --> 00:02:56,005
Type: find ~ -name "*.conf".

51
00:02:56,005 --> 00:03:00,345
The tilde means search my whole home folder.

52
00:03:00,895 --> 00:03:01,612
One match,

53
00:03:01,612 --> 00:03:03,333
deep inside the project.

54
00:03:03,333 --> 00:03:05,054
The star means anything,

55
00:03:05,054 --> 00:03:08,639
so star dot conf is every name ending in dot conf.

56
00:03:09,189 --> 00:03:14,352
To find folders only, type: find ~ -type d -name config.

57
00:03:14,902 --> 00:03:16,440
Type d means directory.

58
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,714
The next lesson adds more filters,

59
00:03:18,714 --> 00:03:19,316
and grep,

60
00:03:19,316 --> 00:03:20,854
to search inside files.

61
00:03:22,904 --> 00:03:26,104
Vim is on almost every server, so learn its three modes.

62
00:03:26,654 --> 00:03:29,897
Normal mode. Keys are commands. You always start here.

63
00:03:30,447 --> 00:03:34,735
Insert mode. Keys are text. Press i to enter, and Escape to leave.

64
00:03:35,285 --> 00:03:36,381
Command mode.

65
00:03:36,381 --> 00:03:37,478
Type a colon.

66
00:03:37,478 --> 00:03:38,659
Colon w saves.

67
00:03:38,659 --> 00:03:39,840
Colon q quits.

68
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:42,709
Colon q bang quits without saving.

69
00:03:43,259 --> 00:03:46,715
Lost? Press Escape twice. You are back in normal mode.

70
00:03:48,765 --> 00:03:52,008
Open a new file. Type: vim todo.txt.

71
00:03:52,558 --> 00:03:58,104
A blank file. Vim starts in normal mode, where keys are commands, so do not type text yet.

72
00:03:58,654 --> 00:04:03,412
Press i for insert mode. Watch the bottom of the screen. It now says insert.

73
00:04:03,962 --> 00:04:06,415
Now type three lines, for my to do list.

74
00:04:09,465 --> 00:04:13,241
Press Escape. Back to normal mode. The word insert is gone.

75
00:04:13,791 --> 00:04:17,076
Delete a line with d d. Two d's, in normal mode.

76
00:04:18,126 --> 00:04:20,878
Oops. Press u to undo. The line comes back.

77
00:04:21,428 --> 00:04:25,887
Search with a slash. Slash, eggs, Enter. The cursor jumps to the match.

78
00:04:27,437 --> 00:04:30,402
Save and quit with colon, w, q, then Enter.

79
00:04:30,952 --> 00:04:33,960
Prove it saved. Type: cat todo.txt.

80
00:04:34,510 --> 00:04:35,954
Now the escape hatch.

81
00:04:35,954 --> 00:04:36,916
Open the file,

82
00:04:36,916 --> 00:04:37,879
delete a line,

83
00:04:37,879 --> 00:04:38,635
then colon,

84
00:04:38,635 --> 00:04:38,772
q,

85
00:04:38,772 --> 00:04:39,116
bang,

86
00:04:39,116 --> 00:04:40,697
to quit without saving.

87
00:04:43,247 --> 00:04:46,106
Check it. Type: cat todo.txt again.

88
00:04:46,656 --> 00:04:50,816
All three lines are still there. Your change was thrown away, on purpose.

89
00:04:52,866 --> 00:04:54,189
Four myths to clear up.

90
00:04:54,739 --> 00:04:59,005
Myth one: you need an editor to make a file. Touch does it in one command.

91
00:04:59,555 --> 00:05:03,843
Myth two: rm sends files to a trash. No. It deletes for good.

92
00:05:04,393 --> 00:05:09,428
Myth three: vim is broken, because you cannot type. It is in normal mode. Press i.

93
00:05:09,978 --> 00:05:14,394
Myth four: cp copies folders on its own. A folder needs dash r.

94
00:05:14,944 --> 00:05:16,672
Know the mode. Know the flag.

95
00:05:18,722 --> 00:05:21,047
Here is how you check your own understanding.

96
00:05:21,597 --> 00:05:23,133
Create folders and files.

97
00:05:23,683 --> 00:05:26,457
Read a file with cat, head, tail and W C.

98
00:05:27,007 --> 00:05:28,457
Copy, move and rename.

99
00:05:29,007 --> 00:05:30,629
Delete only after looking.

100
00:05:31,179 --> 00:05:32,608
Find a file by name.

101
00:05:33,158 --> 00:05:35,803
Edit and save with vim, and quit without saving.

102
00:05:36,353 --> 00:05:37,975
To pass, you need all six.

103
00:05:40,025 --> 00:05:40,657
Your turn,

104
00:05:40,657 --> 00:05:42,172
in your virtual machine.

105
00:05:42,172 --> 00:05:44,383
Work only inside a practice folder,

106
00:05:44,383 --> 00:05:46,404
so nothing important is at risk.

107
00:05:46,954 --> 00:05:51,114
One: build practice, slash a, slash b, slash c, with a single command.

108
00:05:51,664 --> 00:05:54,821
Two: create three files, and list them with their sizes.

109
00:05:55,371 --> 00:05:59,306
Three: copy slash etc slash hostname into your folder,

110
00:05:59,306 --> 00:06:00,909
then read it with cat,

111
00:06:00,909 --> 00:06:01,856
W C and file.

112
00:06:02,406 --> 00:06:06,716
Four: rename one file, move another, and copy a folder with dash r.

113
00:06:07,266 --> 00:06:12,855
Five: find your dot text files with find, then delete one, only after you have listed it.

114
00:06:13,405 --> 00:06:14,199
Six: in vim,

115
00:06:14,199 --> 00:06:15,390
write three lines,

116
00:06:15,390 --> 00:06:16,317
undo a change,

117
00:06:16,317 --> 00:06:16,647
save,

118
00:06:16,647 --> 00:06:18,037
then reopen the file,

119
00:06:18,037 --> 00:06:18,964
delete a line,

120
00:06:18,964 --> 00:06:20,552
and quit without saving.

121
00:06:21,102 --> 00:06:24,408
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

122
00:06:39,958 --> 00:06:40,833
Quick check.

123
00:06:41,383 --> 00:06:43,601
Which flag makes missing parent folders?

124
00:06:44,151 --> 00:06:45,773
Which flag copies a folder?

125
00:06:46,323 --> 00:06:48,371
Which key starts insert mode in vim?

126
00:06:48,921 --> 00:06:50,691
How do you quit vim without saving?

127
00:06:51,241 --> 00:06:57,641
Objective covered: MC04-O02.

128
00:06:58,191 --> 00:07:00,261
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

129
00:07:10,811 --> 00:07:13,733
Next lesson: pipes, redirects, grep and find.

130
00:07:14,283 --> 00:07:17,611
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
